  "full_response": "If you've unintentionally infringed someone's copyright, the legal actions that can be taken against you depend on several factors, including the jurisdiction, the nature of the infringement, and the copyright holder's response. Here’s a general overview of what could happen and how to address the situation:\n\n### **1. Potential Legal Actions Against You**\n#### **A. Cease and Desist Letter**\n   - The copyright holder (or their lawyer) may send you a **cease and desist letter**, demanding that you:\n     - Stop using the copyrighted material immediately.\n     - Remove the infringing content (if online).\n     - Provide proof of compliance.\n   - This is often the first step before legal action.\n\n#### **B. DMCA Takedown Notice (For Online Content)**\n   - If the infringement is online (e.g., on a website, social media, or YouTube), the copyright holder can file a **DMCA takedown notice** with your hosting provider or platform (e.g., Google, Facebook, YouTube).\n   - The platform may remove your content, and you could face:\n     - A **strike** (e.g., on YouTube or social media).\n     - Temporary or permanent account suspension.\n   - You can **counter-notify** if you believe the takedown was a mistake (but this can escalate legal risks).\n\n#### **C. Lawsuit for Copyright Infringement**\n   - If the copyright holder decides to sue, they may seek:\n     - **Injunctive Relief** – A court order forcing you to stop using the material.\n     - **Actual Damages** – Compensation for financial losses caused by your infringement.\n     - **Statutory Damages** (in the U.S.) – Up to **$30,000 per work** (or **$150,000 per work** if willful infringement is proven, even if unintentional).\n     - **Attorney’s Fees & Court Costs** – You may have to pay the copyright holder’s legal fees.\n   - **Willful vs. Unintentional Infringement**:\n     - If you **didn’t know** you were infringing, damages may be lower (but not always).\n     - If you **should have known** (e.g., using a famous song without checking), courts may treat it as willful.\n\n#### **D. Criminal Charges (Rare for Un",
